{
  // ============================================================================
  // TypeScript — one config for the app, the check scripts and the Vite config
  // ============================================================================
  // The standard Vite React template splits this into tsconfig.app.json +
  // tsconfig.node.json because its "node" half (the Vite config) and its "app" half run in
  // different places. Here they don't: the check scripts under scripts/ import the very same
  // modules the browser does, and any of them running under different lib/target settings than
  // the app is exactly how a check ends up passing on something the build rejects. One file, one
  // answer — the same reason LIMITS and innerRi are single definitions.
  //
  // Two settings carry more weight than the rest, so they are spelled out:
  //
  //   allowImportingTsExtensions — relative imports inside src/ are written WITH their `.ts` /
  //     `.tsx` extension. Vite would happily resolve them without one, but the check scripts are
  //     run by plain `node` (>=22.18, type stripping on by default), and Node does not rewrite a
  //     `.js` specifier onto a `.ts` file — it throws ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND. Writing the real
  //     extension is what lets one set of source files serve both. `noEmit` is its precondition
  //     (tsc must not emit files whose imports point at .ts), and this project never emits:
  //     Vite builds the app, Node strips types for the scripts.
  //
  //   verbatimModuleSyntax — Vite (and Node's stripper) transpile one file at a time, so a
  //     type-only import that looks like a value import is a runtime import of a module that
  //     may not exist at runtime. This forces `import type` where it is meant, which is the
  //     form both transpilers need.
  //
  // Deliberately NOT enabled: noUncheckedIndexedAccess. The geometry is written as index
  // arithmetic over point arrays (`d[i - 1]`, `P[i + 1].t`, `op[i][0]`), where the loop bounds are
  // the proof that the index is in range. Turning it on adds a `!` to a few hundred expressions
  // that are already correct and hides the handful of genuinely optional values behind the noise.
  // ============================================================================
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "preserve",
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",

    "strict": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    // A declaration nothing reads is dead weight the compiler can see for free, and it accumulates
    // quietly: a review turned up 19 of them at once — an unused `React` default import in seven
    // files (the automatic JSX runtime has not needed one since 17), a destructuring list in
    // TomoshibiStudio that had drifted apart from what the file renders, and two locals left behind
    // when a branch was removed. Turned on with that count at zero, so it stays there.
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,

    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true,

    // `vite/client` for the CSS side-effect import and import.meta.env; `node` for the check
    // scripts, which are part of this same compilation (see the note above). Listing them is what
    // gives every file in `include` the same globals — the alternative, the template's split
    // app/node tsconfigs, buys a narrower DOM-vs-node separation for a second place to keep in step.
    "types": ["vite/client", "node"]
  },
  "include": ["src", "scripts", "vite.config.ts"]
}
